{"title":"Thin-film dewetting and coating","markdown":{"headingText":"Thin-film dewetting and coating","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-thin-film","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n`apps/thin_film` — conserved fourth-order lubrication flow.\n\n## Physical setting\n\nA thin liquid film on a solid substrate. Two effects compete: surface tension\nflattens the free surface, and van der Waals interaction with the substrate\neither thins or thickens the film depending on its local height. Whether a\ncoating levels into a uniform layer or breaks up into droplets is decided by\nwhich one wins at which wavelength — a question with obvious relevance to\ncoating processes and to the stability of deposited layers.\n\n## Governing equations\n\nOne conserved thickness field $h$ with constant mobility $M_0$:\n\n$$\n\\partial_t h \\;=\\; \\nabla\\cdot\\big[M_0\\nabla p\\big],\n\\qquad\np \\;=\\; -\\gamma\\nabla^2 h \\;-\\; \\Pi(h),\n$$ {#eq-tf-pde}\n\nwith the disjoining pressure\n\n$$\n\\Pi(h) \\;=\\; A\\left[\\left(\\frac{h_0}{h}\\right)^{3}\n                   -\\left(\\frac{h_0}{h}\\right)^{9}\\right].\n$$ {#eq-tf-disjoining}\n\n::: {.callout-warning}\n## Sign convention\n\nSome textbook flux forms carry a leading minus that would make the capillary\n$k^4$ term *destabilising*. @eq-tf-pde is the standard lubrication balance —\nflow down the pressure gradient — so short wavelengths damp, which is the\nphysically correct behaviour.\n:::\n\nCubic mobility $M \\propto h^3$ is a local-flux nonlinearity and is deliberately\nleft out of this slice; $M_0 = M(h_0)$ is exact for the linear band, which is\nwhat the verification targets.\n\n## Spectral formulation\n\nWith $u = k_{\\mathrm{lap}}$,\n\n$$\nL(k) \\;=\\; -M_0\\gamma\\,u^2 \\;-\\; M_0\\,\\Pi'(h_0)\\,u ,\n\\qquad\nM_{\\mathrm{nl}}(k) \\;=\\; -M_0\\,u .\n$$ {#eq-tf-symbol}\n\nIn terms of $|\\mathbf{k}| = k$ the linear growth rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda(k) \\;=\\; M_0 k^2\\big(\\Pi'(h_0) - \\gamma k^2\\big),\n$$ {#eq-tf-growth}\n\nwhich is the whole story of the model in one line. The $-\\gamma k^4$ term is\ncapillary damping and always stabilises. The $+\\Pi'(h_0)k^2$ term destabilises\nwhen $\\Pi'(h_0) > 0$. Their balance selects a fastest-growing wavelength\n$k_{\\max}^2 = \\Pi'(h_0)/(2\\gamma)$, which is the observed droplet spacing in\ndewetting.\n\nSetting $A = 0$ removes the disjoining pressure entirely and leaves\n$\\lambda(k) = -M_0\\gamma k^4$: every mode decays, and the film levels. That is\nthe coating limit, and it is a clean analytical target because the decay is\nexact.\n\n## Solution strategy\n\nETD1 as in @eq-etd1, dealiased.\n\n## Verification\n\n* **Exact $k^4$ levelling.** With $A=0$, a single mode decays as\n  $e^{-M_0\\gamma k^4 t}$.\n* **Two-mode ratio.** The relative decay of two modes matches the $k^4$\n  prediction.\n* **Dewetting band.** With $A>0$, modes inside the unstable band grow and\n  those outside decay, with the crossover at the predicted wave number.\n* **Mass conservation.** Mean thickness is preserved.\n\n## Binaries and inputs\n\n`thin_film` (CPU), `thin_film_hip`. Presets: `leveling.json`,\n`dewetting.json`.\n\n## Scalability\n\nNot measured.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-l